Scout Mastership in 7 Minutes or less.

33
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:56,000
What's a scout master?

34
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Look at the term for a moment. I mean it's a good question.

35
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
What is a scout master?

36
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,000
In the first part of the last century, when scouting was founded in Great Britain by Baden-Powell,

37
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
the term for a teacher was school master

38
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:20,000
And so Baden-Powell kind of adopted it to scouting, called us scout masters

39
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:27,000
And that term crossed the pond when scouting did and it was adopted here in the United States.

40
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:36,000
But in the intervening century the cultural and kind of societal look at what a scout master is

41
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
has been fairly inaccurate

42
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:49,000
And I think that those kind of cultural references sometimes make us into something that we're not.

43
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:57,000
I mean, if you see a scout master depicted in the broader culture in a cartoon or a movie or something like that,

44
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:03,000
he's kind of the guy at the head of the column with the flag leading a group of tiny little scouts through the woods

45
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:10,000
And you know they look up to him and he does all of the leading and instructing and things like that.

46
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,000
But really scouting isn't about that.

47
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Scouting is not about adults leading a bunch of boys through a series of activities

48
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
And that is not the job of a scout master.

49
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
A scout master is a facilitator.

50
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
A scout master is a servant.

51
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
A scout master is a trainer, a mentor.

52
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
A scout master is a coach.

53
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:39,000
I would probably think the best way to put it in our particular culture,

54
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
the most accurate way to put it, would be that we're not scout masters, we're scout coaches,

55
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
And I think that would be a lot more relevant.

56
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,000
So we're not going to actually change the term,

57
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
But just in the way of thinking about it and answering the question: what is a scout master?

58
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Start thinking about yourself as a coach.

59
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,000
What are the key elements of being an effective scout master or an effective scout coach?

60
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Well, you do have to work well with boys and you do have to care about them,

61
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
And then you have to teach and coach them how to do things for themselves,

62
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:20,000
And the only way we can do that is by understanding the scouting program, the scouting process, you know,

63
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
And we need to be a reasonably good example, and we need to be comfortable in the outdoors,

64
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
because we're going to spend a lot of time outdoors.

65
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:32,000
So there are basically three things that we're talking about.

66
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:39,000
A scout master has to be some things, he has to know some things and he has to do some things.

67
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,000
Now, to start out with, a scout master only needs to be a few things.

68
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
He needs to be a decent role model.

69
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
You can't be perfect-

70
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,000
And I understand that, because I'm not perfect, neither are you-

71
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
But we must set an example, because our example is going to be followed,

72
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
and it's going to be followed unconsciously, and it is unavoidable.

73
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:05,000
What you do is important to your scouts and they will follow your example.

74
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
We're going to need to be approachable and we're going to need to be affable

75
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
and we're going to need to have a good sense of humor and we're going to need to be friendly,

76
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Because a big, cranky kind of scout master who just barks and hollers at people all the time

77
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
boys aren't going to deal with that,

78
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
They're going to walk away.

79
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,000
And we need to be a good example of the scouting program.

80
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
I mean, there are rules in scouting and there are procedures and policies

81
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
and we need to be loyal and we need to follow those things.

82
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
That's where you start.

83
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,000
That's what you have to be.

84
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,000
You don't have to be highly skilled,

85
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
You don't have to have a great deal of experience and knowledge about scouting,

86
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
but you need to be those things

87
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
And then you can move on to what you need to know.

88
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:59,000
We need to have a lot of confidence in the scouting program.

89
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Now, the scouting program, as it has been laid out through the Boy Scouts of America,

90
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
has been around now for a century.

91
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,000
It's changed, it's altered, it's been refined, and everything

92
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:16,000
but the basic tenets, the basic core of what scouting is,

93
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,000
has not really changed all that much,

94
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:25,000
And that basic core is that boys are given the responsibility

95
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
for being leaders of their troop.

96
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
That is the core, at the heart of scouting,

97
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
And we need to know that,

98
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
and we need to know that the best way for things to work

99
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,000
is by using patrols and by leadership responsibilities.

100
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:48,000
whole, real, actual leadership responsibilities are given to our scouts.

101
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:53,000
We have to be fairly confident that we can develop the skills

102
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:59,000
to make this happen and we have to know that we are not alone,

103
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
That there are plenty of resources out there

104
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
and they're very valuable resources.

105
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,000
There are other scout leaders just like us.

106
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
There are training opportunities.

107
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,000
There is just a sea of literature out there.

108
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,000
There are things like this podcast and scoutmastercgcom

109
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
that may assist you.

110
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
There is a lot of help out there.

111
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:27,000
I would say that that knowledge also infers that we are going to need help.

112
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
We're not going to do this on our own,

113
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:34,000
So it's important to know that there's a lot of help and support out there

114
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:39,000
and it's important to be ready to accept that help.

115
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
Now, what does a scoutmaster do?

116
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:46,000
Well, that's a subject for a lot of talk.

117
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:50,000
I mean, we could spend a lot of time talking about that,

118
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
but let's boil it down to a couple of things.

119
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:58,000
We need to help our scouts develop confident leadership skills

120
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
and we do this through coaching and mentoring

121
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:06,000
and making sure that we keep the responsibility where it belongs,

122
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:08,000
right there with the scouts,

123
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,000
And we have to have fun doing that.

124
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:15,000
I mean, if it becomes a chore- and it does at times,

125
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:20,000
if it becomes kind of a dead obligation- and it does at times-

126
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
we lose a lot of enthusiasm.

127
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:27,000
But we have to really infuse that enthusiasm into our scouts

128
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:31,000
and there's got to be a lot of good energy and a lot of fun around what we do.

129
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,000
If all this sounds like a big, big challenge, don't worry.

130
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
People have been doing it for a hundred years

131
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
and when people are successful at this,

132
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:44,000
it is because they are adhering to the core principles

133
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
of what a scoutmaster must be,

134
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
what a scoutmaster must know

135
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
and what a scoutmaster must do.

136
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
What's at the heart of our mission?

137
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Well, the BSA puts it this way:

138
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:01,000
we're preparing young people to make ethical and moral choices over their lifetimes

139
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,000
by instilling in them the values of the scout, oath and law-

140
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:09,000
And you know, there's probably 700 different ways of saying that,

141
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
but I think that's a pretty decent way of saying it-

142
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:15,000
And we do this through an active, well-rounded scouting program

143
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
and by understanding our roles.

