That's right.

176
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
Now, a lot of times we think

177
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,000
the way to the brain, goes through the ears,

178
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:48,000
but not so with Scouts.

179
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Scouts like to get their hands on things.

180
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:51,000
They like to try things out.

181
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,000
They don't want to listen to you talk about things.

182
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:57,000
You know, a big part of the process of leaving childhood

183
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:01,000
is, sadly, learning to stand and listen politely.

184
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
Standing and listening politely is not natural for boys.

185
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
It's not natural.

186
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
Many concepts only become real to us at that age

187
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
by trying things out, getting hold of them,

188
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,000
rather than just quietly observing or listening.

189
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
Scouting's a verb,

190
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
It's active, engaged, it's moving.

191
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,000
It's not something to watch, it's something to do.

192
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:25,000
Scouting goes through the hands, into the brain

193
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
and into the heart as well.

194
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:30,000
So are we at our best when we're telling,

195
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
when we're shelling or when we're doing?

196
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
To my mind, it's doing

197
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
The direct experience,

198
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
Getting your hands on stuff,

199
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Trying things out.

200
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
That's the most important part of our work

201
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
and the most valuable.

